steps.content:
You are mapping the complete externally influenced attack surface of {{repo_full}} at commit {{commit_sha}}.

Repository scope: {{repo_scope}}
Dependencies available to this run: {{dependencies}}
Runtime configuration: {{configuration}}

Enumerate each distinct production entrypoint through which an untrusted or less-trusted actor can directly or indirectly influence execution or persistent state. Cover public HTTP, GraphQL, gRPC, RPC, WebSocket, webhook, peer and validator protocols, transaction and message handlers, block and consensus callbacks, queue/event consumers, parsers and decoders, snapshot/import paths, public contract functions, callbacks, upgrade and governance handlers, and public package or CLI boundaries when their input reaches production state. Include inputs written in one operation and consumed later by another component.

For every surface, prove the concrete ingress or registration path with ordered path:line:symbol evidence. Name the least-privileged realistic actor, every controlled field or sequence element, and each trust boundary crossed. Follow production wiring, interfaces, generated adapters, dispatch tables, configuration defaults, and workspace dependencies. Do not silently treat a referenced dependency as safe: if its source is absent, record that fact in the reachability evidence while preserving the concrete caller surface for later review.

Return one result per handler or semantically distinct callback. Do not merge routes merely because they share a dispatcher. Exclude tests, examples, benchmarks, dead code, and interfaces proven limited to a trusted local process. Do not hunt vulnerabilities at this stage. Return a stub only after systematic enumeration finds no production surface.

Trace every materially distinct production flow reachable from this surface in {{repo_full}} at {{commit_sha}}.

Surface: {{surface_label}} ({{surface_kind}})
Handler: {{surface_handler}} at {{surface_file_path}}:{{surface_line}}
Actor: {{surface_external_actor}}
Controlled inputs: {{surface_controlled_inputs}}
Reachability: {{surface_reachability_evidence}}
Trust boundaries: {{surface_trust_boundaries}}

For each flow, follow the controlled input through parsing and normalization, identity or domain binding, validation, authorization, dispatch, state reads, state transitions, external or cross-component calls, persistence, and the terminal action. Include deferred consumption, callbacks, retries, replays, failure and rollback paths, multi-stage protocols, consensus phases, and interactions where a value validated by one component is interpreted again by another. Separate flows whenever a branch changes a guard, state transition, privilege, sink, failure mode, or security invariant.

State the important pre-state and post-state. List the security invariants the implementation relies on, the exact guards and assumptions intended to preserve them, and adversarial alternate sequences such as skip, repeat, reorder, race, replay, partial completion, malformed boundary value, non-canonical representation, stale state, or cross-session reuse. Mark unavailable dependency source in flow_source_gaps; do not replace missing code with an assumption that it is safe.

Provide an ordered flow_trace of path:line:symbol hops grounded in source. Return one result per distinct flow. Do not decide bounty eligibility and do not report vulnerabilities yet.

Adversarially investigate this exact production flow for concrete identity, authorization, replay, and state-machine vulnerabilities.

Surface: {{surface_label}} at {{surface_file_path}}:{{surface_line}}
Actor and input: {{surface_external_actor}} controls {{surface_controlled_inputs}}
Flow: {{flow_summary}}
Trace: {{flow_trace}}
Pre-state: {{flow_state_before}}
Post-state: {{flow_state_after}}
Invariants: {{flow_security_invariants}}
Guards and assumptions: {{flow_guards_and_assumptions}}
Alternate sequences: {{flow_alternate_sequences}}
Source gaps: {{flow_source_gaps}}

Try to violate the stated invariants through missing or inconsistent binding between actor, account, session, chain/domain, object, request, proof, nonce, phase, or state. Test stage skipping, repeated or out-of-order messages, replay across sessions or domains, stale authorization, confused deputy behavior, duplicate acceptance, inconsistent validation between components, alternate encodings, and checks that validate a weaker property than the later operation assumes. Trace both the normal path and the shortest adversarial sequence.

Emit every distinct code-grounded technical vulnerability even when severity, payout eligibility, deployment status, or a deterministic PoC still needs later validation. Set exploitable=true only when the actor-controlled end-to-end trigger is established. When a concrete faulty operation and reachable trigger are established but one material fact is missing, set exploitable=false and state that evidence gap precisely in explanation; do not discard it solely for that reason. Never invent behavior in unavailable source. Return a stub only when this lens finds no concrete vulnerable operation in this flow. Point file_path and line to the faulty operation and make trigger_flow an ordered path:line:symbol sequence from ingress to effect.

Adversarially investigate this exact production flow for concrete parsing, decoding, canonicalization, memory-safety boundary, and crash vulnerabilities.

Surface: {{surface_label}} at {{surface_file_path}}:{{surface_line}}
Actor and input: {{surface_external_actor}} controls {{surface_controlled_inputs}}
Flow: {{flow_summary}}
Trace: {{flow_trace}}
Terminal action: {{flow_terminal_action}} at {{flow_file_path}}:{{flow_line}}
Input at sink: {{flow_input_at_sink}}
Invariants: {{flow_security_invariants}}
Guards and assumptions: {{flow_guards_and_assumptions}}
Source gaps: {{flow_source_gaps}}

Inspect length and offset arithmetic, integer conversions, empty and truncated values, trailing or unused bits, non-canonical encodings, duplicate fields, malformed but accepted structures, recursion and allocation growth, nil or missing values, unchecked lookup results, index and slice boundaries, assertions, panic/exception/fatal paths, parser differentials, and validate-then-reinterpret gaps. Compare what every guard actually proves with the exact preconditions required by the later read, conversion, allocation, decode, dereference, or assertion. Consider values persisted earlier and consumed in a more privileged or consensus-critical phase.

Emit every distinct code-grounded technical vulnerability even when denial-of-service wording, severity, payout eligibility, deployment status, or deterministic reproduction needs later validation. A remotely induced node or consensus-process crash is a technical finding and must not be suppressed by a program-impact filter at discovery time. Set exploitable=true only when the actor-controlled end-to-end trigger is established. For a concrete faulty operation with one material evidence gap, set exploitable=false and name the gap in explanation. Never invent unavailable dependency behavior. Return a stub only when this lens finds no concrete vulnerable operation. Point file_path and line to the faulty operation and make trigger_flow an ordered path:line:symbol sequence.

Adversarially investigate this exact production flow for concrete accounting, consensus, ordering, timing, and cross-component invariant violations.

